/* ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   Claude Basic — the sim layer (CB.sim, <cb-sim-*>)              DEVPLAN Stage S1

   Live-view charts for agent-based sims. They differ from the dashboard charts in
   one way that decides everything else: a sim repaints every frame. Domestication
   draws ~25 histogram heatmaps of 200 columns × 20 bins, so these paint a
   <canvas> instead of re-rendering SVG.

   The design rules (Chris, 2026-11-03, from what's wrong with EvoEco):
     · reports are LINES, never numbers that change under your eyes; values are
       on hover, on demand
     · distributions are histogram heatmaps, one column per reporting period
     · time is the only number, and a dial beats a number
     · legends are marks with names, drawn the way the world draws them
     · controls sit under the world (sim.css), never in a column on the left

   The canvas exception. A canvas can't read var(), so the painters (S.paint.*)
   take a RESOLVED palette as an argument. The elements resolve it from the same
   semantic tokens and re-resolve on the cb-theme event. Painters touch no DOM: they
   draw on whatever ctx they're handed, which is how tests/test.mjs runs them under
   Node against a recording stub. Everything above the DOM line is pure.

   Wiring a sim: hand an element the datamanager's OWN arrays, once —
       popChart.data = { series: [{ label: 'Wild', values: dm.wildSeedPop }, …] };
   then never call draw. One shared animation-frame loop repaints a chart when an
   array's length changes (or when the sim calls el.invalidate() after changing
   values in place). If reset() makes new arrays, assign .data again.
   ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */
